#: ../../operations.rst:1270
msgid ""
"``response_timeout`` bounds silence, not the whole exchange. A download "
"of an hour passes as long as the server keeps sending. A slow upload is "
"the client's affair and stays under ``http.idle_timeout``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1272
msgid ""
"When a server cannot be reached, the request moves to another server of "
"the pool. The server received nothing, so this is safe for every method, "
"also for ``POST``. After a byte of the request was sent, we send a "
"request again only when two things hold. We still have all of it: it has "
"no body, or a body of at most ``retry_body`` that fits the request "
"buffer. And sending it twice does no harm: its method is ``GET``, "
"``HEAD``, ``OPTIONS``, ``PUT`` or ``DELETE``. A ``POST`` or a ``PATCH`` "
"whose connection was lost answers 502, because the server may have "
"carried it out. A request never tries the same server twice, and never a "
"drained one. ``retries: 0`` turns failover off, and the client then "
"receives 502 at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1274
msgid ""
"A request to an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 server also moves when the shared "
"session to the server fails before a response head, under the same rule. "
"The session is first opened again to the same server, once. An HTTP/2 "
"server can also say that it did not act on a request, with "
"``REFUSED_STREAM`` or with a graceful ``GOAWAY``. Such a request is sent "
"again whatever its method, when we still have all of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1276
msgid ""
"A request that moved is one line of the access log and one request in the "
"counters, under the server that answered it. Each server it left counts "
"one in ``sibuna_edge_server_failures_total`` and one in "
"``sibuna_edge_server_failovers_total``. The last field of the log line, "
"``retries``, is the number of servers the request left. A redirect and a "
"413 of our own are logged without a server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1278
msgid "Sticky sessions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1281
msgid ""
"An application that keeps a session in the memory of one server needs "
"every request of a client on that server. A pool offers two ways."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1283
msgid ""
"sticky: \"cookie\"\n"
"sticky: { kind: \"cookie\", cookie: \"srv\" }\n"
"sticky: \"source\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1289
msgid ""
"With ``\"cookie\"`` the first response to a client carries a cookie, "
"``sibuna_srv`` unless we name it. The cookie is ``HttpOnly`` and "
"``SameSite=Lax``, and ``Secure`` on a frontend with TLS. Its value is a "
"keyed hash, not the name of the server, so a client cannot aim at a "
"server of its choice. The key comes from ``http.secret_file``. Nodes that "
"share the file accept each other's cookies. Without the file the key is "
"new at each start, and the clients are balanced once more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1291
msgid ""
"With ``\"source\"`` the client address selects the server, without its "
"port. No cookie is set, so this also serves clients that keep no cookies "
"and TCP or UDP pools. When a server leaves, only its clients move, and "
"they return when it returns. All clients behind one NAT address share a "
"server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1293
msgid ""
"A cookie whose server is down, full, ejected or drained gives way: the "
"request is balanced as usual, and the client receives the cookie of the "
"server that answered. With ``\"source\"`` each client of a server that is "
"down, full, ejected or drained has one other server, and returns when its "
"own server does."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1295
msgid ""
"Behind ``trusted_proxies`` the client address of ``\"source\"`` is the "
"one the proxy recorded, as for the access log and the limits. Without "
"that option it is the socket peer, so all requests of one proxy would "
"share a server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1297
msgid "Keeping the sticky table over a restart"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1300
msgid ""
"A pool with ``sticky: \"table\"`` remembers the server of each client in "
"a table in memory, as a later section describes. The table has "
"``global.sticky_entries`` slots, and a restart empties it. The ``spill`` "
"section puts a file under the table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1302
msgid ""
"spill: {\n"
"    path: \"/var/lib/sibuna/hints.spill\",\n"
"    size: \"64m\",\n"
"},"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1309
msgid ""
"The section stands beside ``global`` and ``frontends``, and both members "
"are required. ``size`` is the size of the file, from ``64k`` to ``64g``. "
"Each client takes 64 bytes, and ``64m`` holds a million slots. A file "
"with room for four times the clients we expect keeps nearly all of them: "
"at twice the clients, three in a hundred find their part of the file "
"full. A change of the section needs a restart. Without the section "
"nothing changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1311
msgid ""
"Every choice that the table learns is offered to the file. When the table "
"has no entry for a client, the edge asks the file and serves the flow by "
"the balanced choice meanwhile. The answer fills the table, and the next "
"flow of the client goes to the remembered server. So clients that the "
"table could not hold, and all clients after a restart or a crash of the "
"daemon, find their server again one flow late."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1313
msgid ""
"What is in the file is a hint and nothing more. The pool uses it only "
"when the server it names may serve. A file that is damaged costs the "
"hints of the damaged part. A file from another version or of another size "
"is replaced by an empty one. The daemon never overwrites a file that is "
"not a spill cache: it refuses to start and names the file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1315
msgid ""
"The daemon says what it found when it starts, and ``show info`` has the "
"counters. ``show affinity`` shows the table in memory as before."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1317
msgid ""
"EDGE015: spill cache /var/lib/sibuna/hints.spill: reused, 1048064 slots "
"in 67084288 bytes on disk, 4425712 bytes of memory beside the table\n"
"\n"
"$ sibuna ctl /run/sibuna/edge.sock show info\n"
"spill: /var/lib/sibuna/hints.spill\n"
"spill_state: reused\n"
"spill_slots: 1048064\n"
"spill_file_bytes: 67084288\n"
"spill_memory_bytes: 4425712\n"
"spill_table_slots: 65536\n"
"spill_asked: 1203\n"
"spill_promoted: 1100\n"
"spill_preferred: 40\n"
"spill_disk_lookups: 1203\n"
"spill_disk_hits: 1100\n"
"spill_disk_written: 1350\n"
"spill_disk_rejected: 4\n"
"spill_disk_corrupt: 0\n"
"spill_disk_shed: 0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1338
msgid ""
"``spill_asked`` counts the lookups that found nothing in memory and asked "
"the file. ``spill_promoted`` counts the choices that the file gave back "
"to memory, and ``spill_preferred`` those of them that replaced a choice "
"made while the file was asked. ``spill_disk_rejected`` counts choices "
"that a full part of the file did not admit: when it grows fast, the file "
"is too small. ``spill_disk_corrupt`` counts slots that failed their check "
"and were cleared. ``spill_disk_shed`` counts work that was dropped "
"because the storage thread was behind: the edge never waits for the disk."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1340
msgid ""
"Keep the file on a local disk, in a directory that only the daemon "
"writes. A second daemon that finds the file in use, as during the "
"replacement of the binary, runs with the table in memory alone and says "
"so. In a cluster each node has a file of its own. The nodes share the "
"table in memory when ``mesh.replicate_sticky`` is set, and what a node "
"hears from a peer is offered to its file too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1342
msgid ""
"**Memory.** The pages of the file that the daemon has used count as its "
"resident memory, up to the whole file. Without a memory limit the kernel "
"takes them back when other programs need the memory. A memory limit of "
"systemd or of a container counts them. We set such a limit above the size "
"of the file plus the memory of the daemon. With a file of 256 MiB our "
"measuring program reached 13 MiB above the size of the file. Under a "
"limit of 96 MiB the same program was killed by the kernel on one host and "
"ran more than fifty times slower on another. Both hosts use ZFS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1344
msgid ""
"**Disk.** The daemon writes the whole size at the first start, so on most "
"file systems a full disk is found then. A file system that compresses or "
"copies on write, such as ZFS or Btrfs, does not keep that promise: on ZFS "
"with compression a new file of 16 MiB took 141 KiB. When such a file "
"system fills up later, a write to the file ends the daemon with a signal. "
"There we give the file a dataset or a volume of its own with reserved "
"space."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1346
msgid ""
"**In a container.** The spill cache needs no privilege, no capability and "
"no kernel setting: it maps, locks and renames an ordinary file. We ran it "
"in an unprivileged LXC container, with and without a memory limit. We "
"have not yet run it under Docker or Kubernetes. Four settings need care."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1348
msgid ""
"*A path that can be written.* The image and the Helm chart run with a "
"read-only root file system, so ``spill.path`` must lie on a mounted "
"volume, and the user of the daemon, UID 65532 in the image, must be able "
"to write the directory. Otherwise the daemon refuses to start with "
"``SPILL004``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1349
msgid ""
"*A volume that outlives the container.* The file also works in the "
"writable layer of a container and in an ``emptyDir``, but there it is "
"lost with the container or the Pod, and a new file is empty. We use a "
"named volume or a PersistentVolumeClaim. We never put the file on a "
"``tmpfs`` or on an ``emptyDir`` with ``medium: Memory``: the whole file "
"then counts as memory and no restart of the host keeps it. The size of "
"the file counts against the size of the volume, and in an ``emptyDir`` "
"against the limit of ephemeral storage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1350
msgid ""
"*The memory limit.* This is ``--memory`` of Docker and "
"``resources.limits.memory`` of Kubernetes. We set it above the size of "
"the file plus the memory of the daemon, as the paragraph on memory says. "
"In Kubernetes we set the request to the same value, because the used "
"pages count as working memory of the Pod."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1351
msgid ""
"*One file for each replica.* The file is locked. A second replica with "
"the same volume and the same path runs with the table in memory alone. We "
"give each replica a volume of its own, as the volume claim template of a "
"StatefulSet does. A network file system such as NFS is not a local disk, "
"and we have not tested one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1353
msgid "Redirects, headers and body limits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1356
msgid ""
"A frontend carries a small HTTP policy. Each route can state its own, and "
"a route keeps every member that it does not state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1358
msgid "Policy members of a frontend or route"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1365
msgid "``redirect_https``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1367
msgid ""
"``true`` answers every cleartext request with 308 and the same host, path "
"and query under ``https://``. ``301`` uses that status. ``{status: 308, "
"port: 8443}`` names another port. Only on a frontend without ``tls``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1368
msgid "``hsts``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1370
msgid ""
"``true`` adds ``Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000`` to the "
"responses of the servers and to our own answers: a redirect, a challenge "
"page, a refusal and an error. ``{max_age: 600, include_subdomains: true, "
"preload: false}`` sets the parts. Only on a frontend with ``tls``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1371
msgid "``request_headers``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1373
msgid ""
"``{set: {...}, remove: [...]}`` for the request that goes to the server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1374
msgid "``response_headers``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1376
msgid "The same for the response that goes to the client."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1377
#, fuzzy
#| msgid "``mac``"
msgid "``max_body``"
msgstr "``mac``"

#: ../../operations.rst:1379
msgid ""
"The largest request body: a number of bytes, or ``\"64k\"``, ``\"10m\"``, "
"``\"1g\"``. A larger request receives 413, also when its length is not "
"declared and the limit is passed while the body streams. After a 413 we "
"read and discard what the client still sends, for at most 2 seconds and 1 "
"MiB, so that the client can read the answer. ``\"off\"`` on a route lifts "
"the limit of its frontend."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1381
msgid ""
"fn security() -> Map<Value> {\n"
"    return {\n"
"        set: { \"X-Frame-Options\": \"DENY\", \"X-Content-Type-Options\": "
"\"nosniff\" },\n"
"        remove: [\"Server\", \"X-Powered-By\"],\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn edge() -> Map<Value> {\n"
"    return {\n"
"        frontends: {\n"
"            plain: {\n"
"                proto: \"http\",\n"
"                binds: [\"0.0.0.0:80\"],\n"
"                default_backend: \"app\",\n"
"                redirect_https: true,\n"
"                routes: [{ path_prefix: \"/.well-known/acme-challenge/\", "
"backend: \"app\",\n"
"                           redirect_https: false }],\n"
"            },\n"
"            web: {\n"
"                proto: \"http\",\n"
"                binds: [\"0.0.0.0:443\"],\n"
"                tls: { crt: \"/etc/sibuna/certs/\" },\n"
"                default_backend: \"app\",\n"
"                hsts: { max_age: 31536000, include_subdomains: true },\n"
"                max_body: \"10m\",\n"
"                response_headers: security(),\n"
"                request_headers: { set: { \"X-Edge\": \"sibuna\" } },\n"
"                routes: [{ path_prefix: \"/upload/\", backend: \"app\", "
"max_body: \"1g\" }],\n"
"            },\n"
"        },\n"
"        backends: {\n"
"            app: {\n"
"                mode: \"http\",\n"
"                sticky: \"cookie\",\n"
"                balance: { kind: \"round_robin\" },\n"
"                servers: [{ name: \"a1\", addr: \"10.20.0.8:8080\" }, { "
"name: \"a2\", addr: \"10.20.0.9:8080\" }],\n"
"            },\n"
"        },\n"
"    }\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1424
msgid ""
"The redirect is decided before the firewall, so a cleartext request is "
"never challenged first. The route for ``/.well-known/acme-challenge/`` "
"above keeps certificate validation over port 80 working."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1426
msgid ""
"``set`` replaces a field: every field of that name is dropped and ours is "
"added. ``remove`` drops a field. A value is fixed text of at most 1024 "
"printable characters. The checker refuses the names that frame a message, "
"such as ``Host``, ``Content-Length`` and ``Connection``, and on requests "
"the forwarding fields that Sibuna writes itself. A request field that we "
"set or remove is also dropped when the client spells it with ``_`` for "
"``-``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1428
msgid ""
"The edits apply to exchanges with a server. Sibuna's own pages, such as a "
"challenge or a 502, do not carry them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1430
msgid ""
"In JSON the members have the same names: ``\"redirect_https\": true``, "
"``\"max_body\": \"10m\"``, ``\"sticky\": {\"kind\": \"cookie\", "
"\"cookie\": \"srv\"}``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1432
msgid "WebSockets"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1435
msgid ""
"A WebSocket begins as an HTTP request, and Sibuna judges that request "
"like any other: bans, limits, the policy and the semantic firewall apply "
"to the handshake. The offer must be a well-formed version 13 handshake, "
"and the server's 101 must carry the matching accept key. "
"``Sec-WebSocket-Protocol`` and ``Sec-WebSocket-Extensions`` pass "
"unchanged, so subprotocols and compression are negotiated end to end. "
"After the 101 Sibuna relays bytes and does not parse frames. A tunnel "
"costs a record and two sockets, not a thread."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1437
msgid ""
"A frontend or a route may carry a ``websocket`` object. A route without "
"one keeps the rule of its frontend."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1439
msgid ""
"{\"routes\": [{\"path_prefix\": \"/api/live\", \"backend\": \"app\",\n"
"             \"websocket\": {\"origins\": [\"https://app.example.org\"], "
"\"max_per_ip\": 16}}]}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1444
msgid "WebSocket rule"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1450
#, fuzzy
#| msgid "``policies``"
msgid "``origins``"
msgstr "``policies``"

#: ../../operations.rst:1451
msgid ""
"The origins that may open a tunnel, each a scheme, a host and an optional "
"port. An upgrade whose ``Origin`` field is absent or not listed answers "
"403. A browser sends a site's cookies with a handshake from any page, so "
"an application that authenticates by cookie needs this check."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1452
msgid "``max_per_ip``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1453
msgid ""
"The most concurrent tunnels of one client address under this rule. A "
"client over it answers 429. A reload starts the count again for new "
"tunnels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1455
msgid ""
"Both checks apply whether or not the route uses the firewall. A browser "
"cannot solve a challenge inside a WebSocket handshake. If a policy "
"challenges the page, the page's session cookie admits the handshake. For "
"a client that is not a browser we allow the tunnel's path in the policy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1457
msgid ""
"Three timers matter. ``websocket_idle_timeout`` closes a tunnel on which "
"neither side sent a byte for that long, so we set it above the "
"application's ping interval. ``idle_timeout`` bounds the handshake. A "
"shutdown closes tunnels after the drain period of five seconds, and "
"Sibuna sends no close frame, because it does not speak the framing. "
"Clients must reconnect, as they must after any network fault."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1459
msgid ""
"A tunnel stays on the server that accepted it. An application that keeps "
"its live state in one process, such as a hub of connected clients, "
"therefore needs a pool with one server, or a broker behind its servers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1461
msgid "TCP inspection and PROXY v2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1464
msgid ""
"A frontend may set ``\"tcp_inspect\": \"tls_client_hello\"``, define "
"``acls`` with exact ``sni`` or ``alpn`` matches or a ``ja4_prefix`` "
"pattern such as ``t13d*``, and list ordered ``deny`` or ``use_backend`` "
"rules. By default the worker closes malformed or incomplete handshakes "
"after a bounded inspection of up to 32 KiB and a default deadline of two "
"seconds, and ``inspect_fallback`` can forward them instead (see the next "
"section). A complete hello with no matching rule uses the default pool. "
"Enable ``\"proxy_v2\": {\"enabled\": true}`` on a TCP pool only when its "
"servers accept a PROXY v2 address header before the stream. We can add "
"``\"tlv_sni\": true`` or ``\"tlv_ja4\": true`` inside ``proxy_v2`` to "
"forward inspected metadata. SNI uses the AUTHORITY type (0x02). Sibuna "
"uses the custom type 0xe0 for the 36 ASCII bytes of JA4. The origin must "
"agree on this type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1466
msgid "Admission at the socket edge"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1469
msgid ""
"A connection or a datagram passes a sequence of checks before it costs a "
"server anything. Each frontend applies the checks of its protocol. SID "
"0032 specifies the three that this section describes. All of them are off "
"until the file names them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1476
msgid "Frontend"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:1478
msgid "``\"defer_accept\": \"5s\"``"
