#: ../../operations.rst:2428
msgid ""
"*Writing a module.* A module exports its memory and six functions, and "
"receives each request as one encoded record in a buffer of its own. The "
"crate ``cmd/sibuna-hook-wasm`` is a complete example in Rust without "
"``std``, and SID 0030 specifies the record and the functions. We build it "
"with ``cargo build -p sibuna-hook-wasm --target wasm32-unknown-unknown "
"--profile wasm``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2430
msgid ""
"*Writing an agent.* An agent accepts connections on its socket and reads "
"frames: a length of four bytes, a version, a kind, and an identifier of "
"eight bytes. It answers a ``decide`` frame with a ``verdict`` frame of "
"the same identifier, whose payload is one byte, 0 to admit, 1 to deny, 2 "
"to challenge, and then the field lines to add. A ``sample`` frame has no "
"answer. SID 0030 specifies the frame. The operator console of the edge "
"~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2434
msgid ""
"The edge can run the console of Part XI beside the load balancer (SID "
"0031). We add a ``console`` section to the topology file. The console "
"listens on an address of its own, on threads of its own, and it keeps its "
"accounts and its audit log in a SQLite file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2436
msgid ""
"console: {\n"
"    listen: \"127.0.0.1:9443\",\n"
"    data_dir: \"/var/lib/sibuna\",\n"
"    node: 1,\n"
"},"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2444
msgid ""
"sibuna init-admin admin --data-dir /var/lib/sibuna    # once, while the "
"daemon is stopped\n"
"sibuna --config-file /etc/sibuna/edge.jlv\n"
"# Console: http://127.0.0.1:9443/console/"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2450
msgid ""
"We sign in, replace the temporary password, and open the page *Edge*. The "
"page shows four things."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2452
msgid ""
"**The node.** Its state is one of three words. *Serving* means that the "
"node admits new flows. *Draining* means that the node was told to stop: "
"it refuses new flows and finishes the open ones. *Full* means that the "
"flow table holds ``global.max_flows`` flows: the node refuses new flows "
"for lack of capacity. The table below the state gives the active flows "
"and the limit, the drops, the servers that are drained or down, the "
"reloads, the routing regime of the mesh and two revisions. The first "
"revision is the digest of the file, the one that ``show cluster`` prints. "
"The second is the revision of the database that the node serves in "
"persistent mode (SID 0028). A node that serves a file has none, and the "
"page says \"Not reported\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2454
msgid ""
"**The refusals by cause.** One row for each reason of the metrics: "
"``flow_limit``, ``draining``, ``rate_limit``, ``connection_limit``, "
"``task_limit`` and the others. A node that refuses for lack of capacity "
"counts under ``flow_limit``, and a node that drains counts under "
"``draining``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2456
msgid ""
"**The frontends and the pools.** A frontend shows its requests, flows, "
"bytes and the latency of a request: the mean and the 95th percentile. A "
"pool shows the columns of ``show servers`` for each server, then its "
"requests, failures, requests in flight and the wait for the first byte. A "
"view carries at most 256 servers, and the page says how many it left out."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2458
msgid ""
"**The fleet.** One row for each member: the state of the link, the state "
"of the node, its flows, its servers and its two revisions. A member whose "
"file differs from the file of this node is marked ``differs``. A member "
"that does not answer in five seconds has the row ``No answer``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2460
msgid ""
"An operator or an administrator also sees the commands. Each command is "
"one line of the admin socket, and the function of the admin socket runs "
"it. The same parser checks it, the same worker applies it, and the same "
"limit of 20 commands in a burst and 10 per second holds. A change reaches "
"the other nodes over the mesh, as it does from ``sibuna ctl``, unless we "
"tick *This node only*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2466
msgid "The console offers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2467
msgid "It does not offer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2468
msgid ""
"``set server POOL/NAME state ready|drain [local]``, ``set server "
"POOL/NAME weight N [local]``, ``add server``, ``del server``, ``del "
"ban``, ``reload cluster``, ``show servers``, ``show cluster``, ``show "
"certs``, ``show ca``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2469
msgid ""
"``reload``, ``reopen log``, ``show info``, ``show stats``, ``show "
"authority``, ``show affinity``, ``revoke cert`` and every other command. "
"They stay on the host, with ``sibuna ctl``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2471
msgid ""
"Every request passes the checks of the console: the session, the role, "
"the ``Origin`` field, the CSRF token and the allowance of the session. A "
"viewer reads the page and changes nothing. The audit log records each "
"change twice: the line before it runs, and the result after. A line that "
"the console does not offer is answered ``CONSOLEEDGE``, and the edge "
"never sees it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2473
msgid ""
"**A fleet.** For more than one node we write the list of all members, the "
"same on every node, so that ``reload cluster`` can carry the file. Each "
"node finds itself by its number, which a small file on the node holds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2475
msgid ""
"console: {\n"
"    data_dir: \"/var/lib/sibuna\",\n"
"    node_file: \"/etc/sibuna/console.node\",\n"
"    behind_proxy: true,\n"
"    trusted_proxies: [\"10.99.0.2/32\"],\n"
"    allow_operators: [\"10.99.10.0/24\"],\n"
"    key_file: \"/etc/sibuna/console.key\",\n"
"    peer_key_file: \"/etc/sibuna/console-peer.key\",\n"
"    peer_ca_file: \"/etc/sibuna/management-ca.pem\",\n"
"    nodes: [\n"
"        { node: 1, listen: \"10.99.0.11:9443\", origin: "
"\"https://fra-edge-01.internal\", allow_ips: [\"10.99.0.11/32\"] },\n"
"        { node: 2, listen: \"10.99.0.12:9443\", origin: "
"\"https://fra-edge-02.internal\", allow_ips: [\"10.99.0.12/32\"] },\n"
"    ],\n"
"},"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2492
msgid ""
"A fleet has at most 64 members. The consoles talk to each other over "
"HTTPS, so each one stands behind a TLS proxy, as Part XI describes for "
"peers. The proxy must set ``X-Forwarded-For``. The two key files hold 64 "
"hexadecimal digits each and are readable by their owner only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2494
msgid ""
"The listener screens a connection by its source address when it accepts "
"it. Behind a proxy it serves the trusted proxies only. Without a proxy, "
"and with ``allow_operators``, it serves those networks only. A screened "
"connection is closed before it takes a slot. A peer must connect from the "
"``allow_ips`` of its entry, must name itself and this node in a fresh "
"transcript, and must prove the peer key. ``allow_operators`` holds for "
"every other request."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2496
msgid ""
"The listener has a fixed number of slots: 64 browser streams, one peer "
"stream for each peer and at least 16, and 16 slots that only ordinary "
"requests may take. A stream over its quota is answered 503, and the page "
"and the commands still work. The console never takes a lock of a request "
"path, and the edge worker looks at one flag for it in each turn."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2498
msgid ""
"**Removing a member.** We delete its entry from the file and reload. The "
"node closes the stream of that member within two seconds and refuses its "
"next connection, although the member still holds the peer key. A new "
"member, or a changed entry, is used after a restart: the daemon logs "
"``EDGE014``. A reload that changes the listener, the directory, the proxy "
"settings or the keys of the console is refused, and the routes stay."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2500
msgid "Several nodes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2503
msgid ""
"For several nodes we can add a top-level ``mesh`` object with a unicast "
"``listen`` address, a list of ``peers`` as numeric UDP socket addresses, "
"and a ``key_file`` containing the same 32 random binary bytes on each "
"node. We provision this key on the management network and restart nodes "
"when rotating it. Peers exchange authenticated challenges every half "
"second. Pools with no explicit scheduler choose two candidates while "
"standalone and use a deterministic weighted Maglev table once a peer "
"responds. The wide-area regime prefers eligible local servers over "
"servers marked ``\"remote\": true``. At least three missed probe periods "
"expire a peer. The ``nodes`` form lists every node in one list instead, "
"so that all nodes can hold one file (section *One file for the cluster*). "
"Each node makes its own health decisions, and its own admission decisions "
"except for fleet-wide limits (SID 0025). The mesh shares bans and "
"commands, a sticky table and fleet-wide limits, and with ``forward`` it "
"sends the new flows of a client to its owner node (sections above, SID "
"0022 to SID 0026). Keeping an established TCP connection on its node "
"across a routing change still requires upstream flow steering."
msgstr ""

